[Music]  all right so here are a few warm-ups  that you can do anywhere  especially if you have a little keyboard  all you need is three octaves  [Music]  we're first going to start off with a  basic major triad  now using the thumb to spot we're going  to go up a tritone and play a pair of  chords that has a 1 2 5 grip  a semi tone apart  [Music]  now going back down a tritone we're  going to play a pair of major triads a  semitone apart  [Music]  the first two chords will be broken  upwards  and the next two will be broken  downwards  [Music]  [Music]  the next one is based off of ascending  augmented triads spaced a whole tone  apart  so what's awkward about this one is that  the thumb and index finger on their own  go up a chromatic scale  [Music]  so it'll be good to get used to how that  feels  as we're playing the augmented triads  broken upwards we're connecting one to  the next with the chromatic scale on the  bottom  [Music]  of course we can try this in reverse  with the left hand  [Music]  the next one can be most efficiently  described using a score so i will supply  that here  [Music]  oh  [Music]  [Music]  and of course i would transpose this in  a few different keys  [Music]  [Music]  let me know if you want to see more of  these because i have a ton of patterns  and exercises like these that i just  come up with on the road to keep my  fingers moving  and also if you haven't checked out  these other videos that i have on  warm-up exercises on a full-size  keyboard definitely go check them out  [Music]  [Music]

